Local LLM prompt pack for Q&A dataset generation
Best use of your local models

Yes — this is a good use case for LM Studio or another local OpenAI-compatible server.

The best pattern is:

    Use your chunked Federalist source text as input.

    Use a local model to generate grounded Q&A pairs from each chunk.

    Save those pairs in chat-training format.

    Fine-tune your Publius chatbot on that generated dataset.

    Later, hand-clean a subset for quality.

Why this is better than templated questions alone

Templated questions are fine for a first proof of concept, but a local model can generate more varied and natural prompts, such as:

    conceptual questions,

    objection-and-reply questions,

    argument summary questions,

    comparison questions,

    implication questions.

That usually gives better chatbot behavior than repeating the same 5 or 6 prompt templates.
Recommended workflow
Option A: fastest

Use the existing build_qna_from_chunks.py file first, train, and validate the loop.
Option B: better dataset

Run generate_qna_with_local_llm.py against your LM Studio server and use that dataset for training instead.
LM Studio setup

In LM Studio:

    Load a decent instruction model.

    Start the local server.

    Confirm the OpenAI-compatible endpoint is active, usually:

        http://localhost:1234/v1

    Edit MODEL in generate_qna_with_local_llm.py to match the loaded model name if needed.

Good model types for generation

Use an instruction-tuned model that is good at structured output and summarization. In practice, strong small-to-medium instruct models are usually enough for this dataset generation step.
Quality advice

Generated Q&A data is useful, but it can also bake in mistakes. So the best pattern is:

    generate a few dozen examples,

    inspect them manually,

    fix obvious garbage,

    then train,

    then expand.

Gold-standard path

The strongest setup is a mixed dataset:

    60 to 80 percent local-model-generated grounded Q&A,

    20 to 40 percent human-reviewed or human-written Q&A.

That usually gives better behavior than either pure templated prompts or pure synthetic data.